1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,802
Every force problem in IB Physics comes
down to two moves: draw the forces,

2
00:00:05,842 --> 00:00:07,652
then split them into components.

3
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:10,788
So where does mg sin θ actually come from?

4
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:15,138
In the next few minutes we'll
derive every contact force you need:

5
00:00:15,178 --> 00:00:18,837
the normal force, friction,
tension, buoyancy and viscous drag.

6
00:00:18,969 --> 00:00:22,015
No springs, no circles, no kinematics.

7
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:26,079
Just forces, triangles, and the
trigonometry that connects them.

8
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:29,456
First, the one tool
we'll use over and over.

9
00:00:29,588 --> 00:00:32,254
A force is an arrow with
a size and a direction,

10
00:00:32,294 --> 00:00:36,579
and any arrow can be replaced by two
perpendicular arrows that add up to it.

11
00:00:37,134 --> 00:00:40,668
Make the force F the hypotenuse
of a right-angled triangle,

12
00:00:40,708 --> 00:00:42,465
at angle θ to the horizontal.

13
00:00:42,597 --> 00:00:47,550
Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, so
the side touching the angle is F cos θ.

14
00:00:47,682 --> 00:00:52,578
Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, so the
side across from the angle is F sin θ.

15
00:00:53,128 --> 00:00:57,126
Swing the angle and watch: as one
component grows, the other shrinks,

16
00:00:57,166 --> 00:00:59,215
but together they always rebuild F.

17
00:00:59,347 --> 00:01:02,466
The rule to remember: cos
goes with the side touching θ,

18
00:01:02,506 --> 00:01:04,649
sin goes with the side across from it.

19
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:08,091
Start simple: a block resting on a table.

20
00:01:08,223 --> 00:01:10,982
Its weight, mg, pulls straight down.

21
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:13,590
The table pushes back up
with the normal force,

22
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:15,987
normal meaning perpendicular
to the surface.

23
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:19,674
Nothing accelerates, so the
vertical forces balance,

24
00:01:19,714 --> 00:01:21,601
and the normal force equals mg.

25
00:01:22,148 --> 00:01:24,535
But these two are not a
Newton's third law pair.

26
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:25,999
Both act on the block.

27
00:01:26,131 --> 00:01:29,100
The partner of the weight is the
block pulling the Earth upward.

28
00:01:29,232 --> 00:01:31,548
And the normal force only equals mg here

29
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:34,122
because nothing else
is pushing up or down.

30
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:38,373
Now pull the block with a rope
at angle θ above the horizontal.

31
00:01:38,506 --> 00:01:44,133
Split the pull: F cos θ drags the block
sideways, and F sin θ lifts it a little.

32
00:01:44,696 --> 00:01:49,952
Vertically, everything still balances:
the normal force plus F sin θ equals mg.

33
00:01:50,084 --> 00:01:53,440
So the normal force is mg minus F sin θ.

34
00:01:53,572 --> 00:01:56,066
Pull harder and the
surface pushes back less.

35
00:01:56,631 --> 00:02:01,395
Push down at the same angle instead, and
the vertical part adds to the weight:

36
00:02:01,435 --> 00:02:03,959
the normal force becomes mg plus F sin θ.

37
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:08,268
Since friction is proportional to the
normal force, pulling up reduces friction,

38
00:02:08,308 --> 00:02:09,889
and pushing down increases it.

39
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,057
The normal force is also what you
feel through your feet in a lift.

40
00:02:14,189 --> 00:02:17,849
Accelerating upward, the floor
must push harder than your weight.

41
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:22,198
Newton's second law gives the
normal force minus mg equals ma,

42
00:02:22,238 --> 00:02:24,984
so the normal force is m times g plus a.

43
00:02:25,545 --> 00:02:29,101
Accelerating downward,
it's m times g minus a.

44
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:30,788
Your weight never changes.

45
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,208
Only the contact force does,
and that's what the scales read.

46
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:38,068
Now the classic: a block
on a slope at angle θ.

47
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:41,970
Gravity doesn't care about the slope, so
the weight still points straight down.

48
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:45,100
But the slope can only push
perpendicular to itself.

49
00:02:45,661 --> 00:02:49,450
So tilt your point of view to
match the slope: one axis along it,

50
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:50,903
one perpendicular to it.

51
00:02:51,035 --> 00:02:54,254
Now the weight is the only force
that isn't lined up with an axis,

52
00:02:54,294 --> 00:02:55,528
so it's the one we split.

53
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:57,532
Here's the key geometry.

54
00:02:57,664 --> 00:03:01,047
Start with the slope flat: its
perpendicular points straight down,

55
00:03:01,087 --> 00:03:02,280
right along the weight.

56
00:03:02,412 --> 00:03:04,140
Now tilt the slope by θ.

57
00:03:04,272 --> 00:03:07,211
Its perpendicular tilts
by exactly the same θ.

58
00:03:07,343 --> 00:03:10,624
So the angle between the weight
and the perpendicular is θ.

59
00:03:11,179 --> 00:03:12,307
Read the triangle.

60
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:16,940
The component touching θ, pressing
into the slope, is mg cos θ.

61
00:03:17,072 --> 00:03:21,666
The component across from θ,
pulling down the slope, is mg sin θ.

62
00:03:21,798 --> 00:03:25,978
Nothing moves into the slope, so
the normal force equals mg cos θ.

63
00:03:26,539 --> 00:03:27,823
Check the extremes.

64
00:03:27,955 --> 00:03:32,252
At zero degrees, sine is zero and
cosine is one: no pull along the slope,

65
00:03:32,292 --> 00:03:34,371
and the full weight on the surface.

66
00:03:34,503 --> 00:03:38,258
At ninety degrees the slope is a
wall: all of the weight acts along it,

67
00:03:38,298 --> 00:03:39,634
and none presses into it.

68
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:41,326
Add friction.

69
00:03:41,458 --> 00:03:45,678
Static friction only pushes as
hard as it needs to, up to a limit:

70
00:03:45,718 --> 00:03:47,396
μs times the normal force.

71
00:03:47,528 --> 00:03:52,064
On the slope, it exactly matches
the down-slope pull, mg sin θ.

72
00:03:52,619 --> 00:03:53,674
Raise the angle.

73
00:03:53,806 --> 00:03:59,895
mg sin θ grows, while the maximum
friction, μs mg cos θ, shrinks.

74
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:01,693
They meet at the tipping point.

75
00:04:02,246 --> 00:04:06,957
Set them equal: mg sin
θ equals μs mg cos θ.

76
00:04:07,089 --> 00:04:11,678
The mass cancels, divide by
cos θ, and μs equals tan θ.

77
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:16,032
Measure the angle where a block just
starts to slip, and you've measured μs.

78
00:04:16,583 --> 00:04:20,607
Once it slides, friction drops
to μd times the normal force,

79
00:04:20,647 --> 00:04:25,253
so the resultant force down the
slope is mg sin θ minus μd mg cos θ.

80
00:04:26,067 --> 00:04:27,074
A trickier one.

81
00:04:27,206 --> 00:04:30,496
Hold a block on a smooth slope
with a horizontal push F.

82
00:04:30,628 --> 00:04:34,309
Now F isn't lined up with either
axis, so it gets split too.

83
00:04:34,866 --> 00:04:37,688
The angle between F and
the slope is also θ.

84
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:42,508
So F cos θ pushes up along the
slope, and F sin θ presses into it.

85
00:04:43,065 --> 00:04:49,618
Along the slope, F cos θ balances
mg sin θ, so F equals mg tan θ.

86
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:54,276
Perpendicular to the slope, the
normal force must balance both:

87
00:04:54,316 --> 00:04:55,911
mg cos θ plus F sin θ.

88
00:04:56,043 --> 00:04:59,745
The push adds to the normal force, so it
can even end up bigger than the weight.

89
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:04,264
Tension is a pull along a string,
directed away from the object.

90
00:05:04,396 --> 00:05:08,061
Hang a mass from a single string
and the tension equals mg.

91
00:05:08,617 --> 00:05:12,677
Now hang a lamp from two strings,
each at angle α to the horizontal.

92
00:05:12,809 --> 00:05:17,418
Split each tension: T cos α
sideways, and T sin α upward.

93
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:19,127
The sideways parts cancel.

94
00:05:19,259 --> 00:05:23,636
The upward parts share the
weight: two T sin α equals mg,

95
00:05:23,676 --> 00:05:26,002
so T equals mg over two sin α.

96
00:05:26,553 --> 00:05:30,726
Flatten the strings and watch: as
α shrinks, sin α heads toward zero,

97
00:05:30,766 --> 00:05:32,354
and the tension shoots up.

98
00:05:32,486 --> 00:05:35,468
That's why you can never pull a
washing line perfectly straight.

99
00:05:36,035 --> 00:05:40,938
With unequal angles, balance each
direction separately: sideways,

100
00:05:40,978 --> 00:05:44,513
T one cos α one equals
T two cos α two; upward,

101
00:05:44,553 --> 00:05:48,128
T one sin α one plus T
two sin α two equals mg.

102
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:50,833
Two equations, two unknown tensions.

103
00:05:50,965 --> 00:05:54,233
Or slide the three forces
nose to tail: in equilibrium,

104
00:05:54,273 --> 00:05:55,898
they close into a triangle.

105
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:01,764
Over a light, smooth pulley, tension
keeps its size and only changes direction.

106
00:06:01,896 --> 00:06:04,313
Tie a block on a table to a hanging mass.

107
00:06:04,445 --> 00:06:08,073
The same tension pulls the block
sideways and holds the hanging mass up.

108
00:06:08,616 --> 00:06:12,189
For the hanging mass,
mg minus T equals ma.

109
00:06:12,321 --> 00:06:14,774
For the block, T equals M a.

110
00:06:14,906 --> 00:06:17,997
The string links them, so they
share the same acceleration.

111
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:21,963
And while it accelerates, the tension
is always less than the hanging weight.

112
00:06:22,767 --> 00:06:24,362
Now put a block in a fluid.

113
00:06:24,494 --> 00:06:26,171
Fluid pressure grows with depth,

114
00:06:26,211 --> 00:06:30,074
so the bottom face is pushed up harder
than the top face is pushed down.

115
00:06:30,206 --> 00:06:31,950
The pushes on the sides cancel.

116
00:06:32,082 --> 00:06:33,599
The difference is buoyancy.

117
00:06:34,145 --> 00:06:37,689
The extra pressure across the
block's height h is ρ g h,

118
00:06:37,729 --> 00:06:39,394
acting on the face area A.

119
00:06:39,526 --> 00:06:44,593
So the buoyant force is ρ g h A,
and since h times A is the volume,

120
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:45,624
that's ρ V g.

121
00:06:45,756 --> 00:06:50,279
Here ρ is the fluid's density, and V
is the volume of fluid pushed aside.

122
00:06:50,411 --> 00:06:53,885
It's exactly the weight of the
fluid that used to fill that space.

123
00:06:54,435 --> 00:06:58,991
A floating object sinks until it
pushes aside its own weight of fluid:

124
00:06:59,031 --> 00:07:02,118
ρ times the submerged
volume times g equals mg.

125
00:07:02,933 --> 00:07:05,306
Move through a fluid, and it drags back.

126
00:07:05,438 --> 00:07:09,541
For a small sphere moving slowly
through smooth, layered flow,

127
00:07:09,581 --> 00:07:12,254
Stokes' law gives the drag: six π η r v.

128
00:07:12,386 --> 00:07:13,659
It grows with speed.

129
00:07:14,206 --> 00:07:15,885
Drop a ball bearing into oil.

130
00:07:16,017 --> 00:07:19,029
Weight pulls down;
buoyancy and drag push up.

131
00:07:19,162 --> 00:07:20,961
As it speeds up, the drag grows,

132
00:07:21,001 --> 00:07:24,103
until the forces balance and
the speed stops changing.

133
00:07:24,235 --> 00:07:25,492
That's terminal speed.

134
00:07:26,054 --> 00:07:27,270
Write the balance:

135
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:32,051
mg equals the buoyant force plus
six π η r times the terminal speed.

136
00:07:32,183 --> 00:07:36,781
Replace each mass with density times
four thirds π r cubed, and solve.

137
00:07:36,913 --> 00:07:41,498
The terminal speed is two r squared
g, times the density difference,

138
00:07:41,538 --> 00:07:42,354
over nine η.

139
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,903
Every contact force
follows the same recipe.

140
00:07:46,035 --> 00:07:49,293
Isolate the object, and draw
only the forces acting on it.

141
00:07:49,425 --> 00:07:51,535
Choose axes that match the surface.

142
00:07:51,668 --> 00:07:55,874
Split each force with its triangle:
cos for the side touching the angle,

143
00:07:55,914 --> 00:07:57,329
sin for the side across.

144
00:07:57,461 --> 00:07:59,049
Then balance each direction.

145
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:02,873
So mg sin θ isn't a formula to memorise.

146
00:08:03,005 --> 00:08:04,877
It's just one side of a triangle.
